align:middle">In the meantime, its stockpile of atomic warheads and missile=
s is growing.</span></li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style=3D"f=
ont:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Lee ar=
gues the world must treat denuclearization as a long-term goal but that the=
 immediate priority must be incentivizing North Korea to freeze its current=
 weapons development. Holding onto maximum pressure while demanding total d=
enuclearization is an =E2=80=9Cidealistic,=E2=80=9D all-or-nothing approach=
 that will only aggravate the crisis, he said.</span></p><p style=3D"font:n=
ormal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Lee is one =
of the first global leaders to say it out loud. But the argument he made is=
 one that=E2=80=99s gaining traction: It=E2=80=99s time to try something ne=
w with North Korea.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,ser=
if;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Sanctions ve=
rsus denuclearization</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,s=
erif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The U.S. sanctions against North Kor=
ea are nearly as old as their troubled relationship.</span></p><p style=3D"=
font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Washi=
ngton first imposed them in 1950, after entering the Korean War on South Ko=
rea=E2=80=99s side. It=E2=80=99s been almost eight decades, but North Korea=
 has yet to buckle under the pressure. Instead, it responded to what it cal=
led =E2=80=9CAmerican hostility=E2=80=9D by conducting six nuclear tests be=
tween 2006 and 2017.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,se=
rif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Under American leadership, the United=
 Nations tightened the squeeze in 2017, banning major North Korean exports,=
 including coal, textiles and labor.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>The added pressure he=
lped bring Kim to talks with Trump. The pair met three times between 2018 a=
nd 2019. North Korea wanted sanctions relief; Washington wanted denucleariz=
ation. But the talks </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/newsletter/=
5YtsJXFnkk3Pmu_h2PmvHQ~~/AAAAARA~/DXjW1bSgO-hnMFjSyRBT-Z8t14fskDIZk9ueXVdnD=
BMD7SUzTw8Ac6hpx-3sTLfX9UOSVcZQb9vq_OGJR0Ko7l2y5-Dfbt6pMFSzzCDk26mxAJG_WouP=
